2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] NDJSON = "Newline‑Delimited JSON". Each logical JSON object is written as a single JSON value
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] followed by a newline. Consumers read one line at a time and JSON‑parse each line independently.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] Why use NDJSON
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Simple framing: newline separates records; no brittle ad‑hoc delimiters like "}\t{".
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Stream friendly: producers can stream records and consumers can parse incrementally without
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] buffering the whole stream.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Interoperable: many tools and parsers understand one JSON object per line (logs, jq, streaming
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] processors).
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Easier error isolation: a single malformed line fails parsing for that record only.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] Format example (one record per line)
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - {"messages":[{"from":"fountain","message":"Hello world"}]}
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - {"messages":[{"from":"bot","message":"Status update"}]}
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] Implementation notes (producer)
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Emit exactly one JSON object per line, then '\n' (use '\n', handle '\r\n' compatibility if
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] needed).
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Avoid embedding unescaped raw newlines inside fields; keep message bodies compact or
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] encode/escape them.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Consider adding a small metadata envelope (timestamp, origin, sig) inside each JSON object.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] Implementation notes (consumer)
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Read/append into a line buffer; split on newline characters; keep any partial trailing bytes for
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] the next read.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - For each complete line: trim whitespace, validate length, JSON.parse, then validate schema and
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] process.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - On parse error: log with context (byte offset, size) and drop or quarantine the line — do not
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] crash the process.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] Operational best practices
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Enforce a max line length (e.g., 64 KB) to prevent memory pressure.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Validate payload shape (JSON Schema) before acting on messages.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Sign lines (HMAC) or include a per-line signature field if the pipe is exposed to untrusted
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] peers.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - Rate-limit and monitor stream throughput and parse errors.
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - If binary blobs are needed, send metadata via NDJSON and upload binaries separately (store
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] reference/URL or base64 only if small).
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] When NDJSON is not enough
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] - If you need to transmit arbitrary binary or multi‑line payloads reliably without encoding, use
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] length‑prefixed framing (prefix each record with a fixed-size integer length) or switch to a binary
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] protocol (gRPC, MessagePack with framing).
2025-09-16 10:56:33 [gpt-5-mini] 
