2025-09-24 10:37:44 [nitro@ryzen5] what are annotations and logprobs?
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] In the Responses API output, each text chunk can include an `annotations` array and a `logprobs`
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] array. Annotations hold extra metadata about that span of text—citations, file references, or
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] moderation flags—so you can link the generated words back to sources or warnings. Logprobs are the
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] per-token log probabilities that bought you that text; they’re useful if you want to inspect model
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] confidence or reconstruct alternative continuations.
2025-09-24 10:37:56 [gpt-5-codex] 
2025-09-24 10:38:35 [nitro@ryzen5] what are possible values of role
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] `annotations` list any inline caveats or citations the model adds to that chunk (for example safety
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] flags or references). Empty array means none were needed. `logprobs` holds the token-level log
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] probabilities; it’s blank unless you ask the API for logprobs in the request.
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] 
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] Within the Responses API, each message object carries a `role`. For input messages you send,
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] permissible roles are `system`, `user`, `assistant`, and `tool`. Messages returned in `output` will
2025-09-24 10:38:41 [gpt-5-codex] have `role: "assistant"`.
