2025-09-25 06:22:09 [nitro@ryzen5] is get# a commodore thing?
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025] Yes – in CBM BASIC V2 “GET#” is the disk/file I/O variant of GET.
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025] Usage example:
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025]     OPEN 1,8,15,"$"
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025]     GET#1,A$   : reads one character from device 8
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025] You still also have plain GET (keyboard/serial) and PUT# for output.
2025-09-25 06:22:16 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:26:06 [nitro@ryzen5] how is your knowledge of playstation original design of serial port
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] I’ve dug into the original PS-1 hardware enough to know that its “Serial I/O” port is not a true
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] ±12 V RS-232 interface but a TTL-level, 8-N-1, half-duplex UART driven straight from the console’s
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] custom I/O ASIC.  Key points:
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] • Connector: proprietary 5-pin mini-DIN on the back of the unit
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025]  – Pin-1: +3.3 V
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025]  – Pin-2: GND
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025]  – Pin-3: TXD (console → device)
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025]  – Pin-4: RXD (device → console)
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025]  – Pin-5: (not connected/unused)
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] • Signaling: 3.3 V CMOS levels
2025-09-25 06:26:29 [o4-mini-2025] • Baud: typically 19 200 bit/s by default (some games/tools bump it to 38 400 bps)
2025-09-25 06:26:30 [o4-mini-2025] • Usage: PocketStation link, cheat devices, dev-kit comms
2025-09-25 06:26:30 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:26:30 [o4-mini-2025] If you need register-level details (the PS1’s UART sits in its CBus/SCIF block) or PCB-tracing
2025-09-25 06:26:30 [o4-mini-2025] notes, let me know.
2025-09-25 06:26:30 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:27:12 [nitro@ryzen5] i have yaroze adapter which i assume uses maxim chip
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] Yes—your Yaroze “Serial I/O” adapter is just a TTL↔RS-232 level-shifter using a Maxim MAX3232 (or
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] MAX232‐compatible) chip plus four 0.1 µF caps.
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] Typical pin mapping:
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] · PS1 side (3.3 V TTL):
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025]  – Serial-TXD (console pin 3) → MAX3232 T1IN
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025]  – Serial-RXD (console pin 4) ← MAX3232 R1OUT
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] · DB-9 side (±12 V RS-232):
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025]  – DB9 pin 2 (RxD) → MAX3232 R2IN
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025]  – DB9 pin 3 (TxD) ← MAX3232 T2OUT
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025]  – DB9 pin 5 (GND) → adapter GND
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] If you need the SO-16 pin-out or want to tap the TTL lines directly (bypassing the MAX chip), just
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] let me know.
2025-09-25 06:27:27 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:34 [nitro@ryzen5] I like the idea of networking vic20 to psx, maybe make a 16K cart?
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] Here’s a rough outline of how you could network a VIC-20 to a PlayStation via its Serial I/O port,
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] with a 16 KB VIC-20 cartridge hosting the client firmware.
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 1. Hardware
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	a. VIC-20 side
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		• Cartridge PCB with a 16 KB EPROM (e.g. AMD AM27C128) mapped into $8000–$BFFF
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		• A minimal RS-232 interface off the user port (use a MAX3232 + 0.1 µF caps) or use the stock
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 6551-based VIC-20 user-port modem adapter
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		• DB-9 cable wired to the PS-1 Serial I/O adapter
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	b. PS-1 side
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		• Use your Yaroze serial adapter (MAX3232)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		• Standard DB-9 → mini-DIN wiring as you already have
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 2. Cartridge ROM layout (16 KB)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• $8000–$8FFF: init & I/O routines
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• $9000–$9FFF: simple UART driver (baud setup, tx/rx byte)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• $A000–$A7FF: packet protocol (framing, checksums)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• $A800–$BFFF: user API & test UI
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 3. Firmware modules (VIC-20)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	function initSerial()
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		– configure 6551 or user-port UART for 19 200 bps, 8-N-1
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	function sendPacket(buffer, length)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		– add start/stop bytes, checksum
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		– loop: txByte()
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	function receivePacket(buffer, maxLen)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		– wait for start byte, read until stop, verify checksum
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 		– return length or error
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 4. PS-1 side
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Homebrew tool (host) that opens the DevKit UART at 19 200 bps
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Implements the same packet protocol
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Example flow: VIC INIT → hand-shake → send/receive data blocks
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 5. Next steps
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Define a small file-transfer or command protocol (e.g. read/write RAM, poke/peek, simple chat)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Prototype on PC ↔ VIC-20 first (so you can debug faster)
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Port the PC host tool to PS-1 once stable
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] That approach gives you a minimal 16 KB cart, leverages existing MAX3232 hardware, and a simple
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] packet layer you can reuse on both ends.
2025-09-25 06:28:45 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:29:55 [nitro@ryzen5] i have a pico raspi which i may use as hub
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] Here’s a high-level plan for using a Raspberry Pi Pico as a serial “hub” between your PS-1, VIC-20
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] and (optionally) a PC:
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] 1. Hardware Wiring
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] 	• PS-1 → Pico UART0 (TTL 3.3 V)
2025-09-25 06:30:08 [o4-mini-2025] 		– PS-1 TXD → Pico GP0 (UART0 RX)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– PS-1 RXD ← Pico GP1 (UART0 TX)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– Common GND
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• VIC-20 → Pico UART1
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– VIC-20 RS-232 ↔ MAX3232 ↔ Pico GP4/GP5 (UART1 TX/RX)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– Common GND
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• (Optional) USB → Pico’s USB-CDC for PC connectivity
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 2. Firmware Architecture
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	a) init_hardware()
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– configure GPIOs, both UARTs at 19 200 bps, 8-N-1
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– set up USB-CDC if needed
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	b) forward_loop()
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– read nonblocking from UART0; if byte(s) available, write to UART1 (or USB)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– read nonblocking from UART1; if byte(s) available, write to UART0 (or USB)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– (optional) apply simple packet framing/checksum filters
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	c) error_handling()
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 		– detect UART overruns or framing errors, toggle an LED or log over USB
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 3. Protocol Options
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Transparent byte-wise bridge (“dumb repeater”)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Framed packets: start byte, length, payload, checksum
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Simple commands: PEEK/POKE, file‐block transfers, chat
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 4. Software Stack Choices
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• C with the Pico SDK (for lowest latency)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• MicroPython (faster to prototype)
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 5. Next Steps
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Breadboard the wiring and test “ping-pong” traffic between two UARTs
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Plug your PC into USB-CDC: use a terminal to see raw bytes from both devices
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 	• Incrementally add framing or command handlers
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] Let me know if you’d like starter code (C or MicroPython) for the Pico’s UART bridging or a simple
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] packet protocol example.
2025-09-25 06:30:09 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 06:31:21 [nitro@ryzen5] definitely a hackaday special
2025-09-25 06:31:28 [o4-mini-2025] Sounds like a perfect Hackaday build! Next steps:
