2025-09-25 07:49:19 [nitro@ryzen5] any md usages where a table can fit width of display
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] Markdown itself doesn’t let you set a table’s width—renderers decide that. If you need a full-width
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] table you have two common work-arounds:
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 1. Embed raw HTML in your MD
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse;">
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]      <thead>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]        <tr><th>Column A</th><th>Column B</th></tr>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]      </thead>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]      <tbody>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]        <tr><td>…</td><td>…</td></tr>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]      </tbody>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    </table>
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    Most Markdown engines will pass this straight through.
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 2. Use a Markdown flavor/extension that supports attribute lists
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    (e.g. Pandoc, kramdown, MultiMarkdown):
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    | Col 1   | Col 2   |
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    | ------- | ------- |
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    | Value 1 | Value 2 |
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    {: style="width:100%"}
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025]    Not all renderers honor it, but it keeps you in “pure” Markdown.
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] If you control the page’s CSS, you can also target tables in a wrapper and force `width:100%;`
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] there.
2025-09-25 07:49:29 [o4-mini-2025] 
