2025-09-29 10:22:11 [nitro@ryzen5] running aws command line tools on a pi5
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] AWS CLI runs well on Raspberry Pi 5. Here are the key steps:
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] • Install:*
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] sudo apt update && sudo apt install awscli
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] • Or install latest version:*
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-aarch64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] unzip awscliv2.zip
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] sudo ./aws/install
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] • Configure:*
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] aws configure
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] The Pi 5's ARM64 architecture is fully supported.
2025-09-29 10:22:19 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:24:42 [nitro@ryzen5] what do i do for key?
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] For `aws configure`, you'll need:
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] • AWS Access Key ID* - Your access key
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] • AWS Secret Access Key* - Your secret key
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] These are created in the AWS Management Console:
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 1. Go to IAM (Identity and Access Management)
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 2. Create a user or select existing one
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 3. Generate access keys under "Security credentials"
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 4. Copy both keys - the secret key is only shown once
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] The region and output format can use defaults if unsure.
2025-09-29 10:24:50 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:24 [nitro@ryzen5] i dont want to enable iam
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] If you don't want to use IAM, you have other AWS authentication options:
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] • IAM Roles* - If running on EC2 or ECS
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] • Identity Center (SSO)* - For temporary credentials
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] • Web Identity Federation* - With Cognito or external providers
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] • Container credentials* - If using EKS
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] However, IAM is the primary authentication method for AWS CLI. Most alternatives still rely on IAM
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] roles or policies in the background.
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] What's your specific concern about IAM?
2025-09-29 10:27:31 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:51 [nitro@ryzen5] IAM roles?
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] IAM Roles allow AWS services to access other AWS services without storing access keys.
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] • Common use cases:*
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] - EC2 Instance Role - Attach role to EC2 instance
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] - Lambda Execution Role - For serverless functions
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] - ECS Task Role - For container tasks
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] • How it works:*
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 1. Create IAM role with required permissions
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 2. Attach role to service (EC2, Lambda, etc.)
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 3. AWS automatically provides temporary credentials
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 4. No access keys needed in your code/config
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] This is more secure than long-term access keys.
2025-09-29 10:27:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:46 [nitro@ryzen5] how to create role
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] • Create IAM Role via AWS Console:*
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 1. Navigate to IAM Console
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Go to IAM > Roles > Create role
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 2. Select trusted entity
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - AWS service (for EC2, Lambda, etc.)
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Another AWS account (cross-account)
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Web identity (Cognito, OIDC)
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 3. Attach permissions policies
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Select existing policies (AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess, etc.)
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Or create custom policy
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 4. Name and create
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Add role name and description
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat]    - Create role
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] • For EC2:*
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] - After creation, attach role to EC2 instance in EC2 console
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] - Instance gets temporary credentials automatically
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] Would you like CLI commands instead?
2025-09-29 10:28:56 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:29:43 [nitro@ryzen5] i am at the IAM dashboard
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] Great! From the IAM dashboard:
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 1. Click "Roles" in the left sidebar
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 2. Click "Create role" button
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 3. Select "AWS service" as trusted entity type
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 4. Choose the service that will use this role (e.g., EC2)
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 5. Click "Next"
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 6. Attach permission policies (select from list)
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 7. Click "Next"
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 8. Enter role name and description
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 9. Click "Create role"
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] The role is now ready to attach to your service.
2025-09-29 10:29:49 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:06 [nitro@ryzen5] this is for limiting access to S3 bucket, what would be suitable? can my server redirect file access from ec2?
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] For S3 bucket access, attach the AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess policy (for read-only) or create a
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] custom policy for specific buckets.
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] • Regarding redirects:* Yes, your server can redirect file access from EC2. Common
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] patterns:
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 1. Presigned URLs - Generate temporary S3 URLs that redirect users
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 2. Proxy through EC2 - Serve files through your EC2 instance
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 3. CloudFront - Use CDN in front of S3 for better performance
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] The IAM role gives your EC2 instance permission to access S3, then you can implement the redirect
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] logic in your application code.
2025-09-29 10:31:13 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:52 [nitro@ryzen5] 2
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] For option 2 (proxy through EC2):
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] • IAM Role Setup:*
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Create role with AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess policy
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Attach role to your EC2 instance
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] • Server Logic:*
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - User requests file from your EC2 server
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Server uses AWS SDK to fetch file from S3
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Server streams file back to user with appropriate headers
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] • Benefits:*
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Full control over access logic
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Can add authentication/authorization
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] - Can modify or process files before serving
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] This keeps S3 private while allowing controlled file access through your EC2 instance.
2025-09-29 10:31:59 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:43 [nitro@ryzen5] cheaper than ec2 storage? by how much ?
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] S3 is significantly cheaper than EBS storage:
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • EBS (EC2 storage):*
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - gp3: ~$0.08/GB-month
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - io2: ~$0.125/GB-month
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • S3 Standard:*
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - Storage: ~$0.023/GB-month
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - Plus data transfer costs
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • Savings:*
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - ~70% cheaper for storage alone
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - Additional savings on backups (S3 has built-in redundancy)
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] • Consider data transfer costs:*
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - EC2 to internet: $0.09/GB
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - S3 to internet: $0.09/GB (same)
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] - S3 to EC2: Free within same region
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] For large storage needs, S3 is much more cost-effective.
2025-09-29 10:32:54 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:33:50 [nitro@ryzen5] hmmm no real savings for my use case, cheapes storage for ec2?
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] For cheapest EC2 storage:
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] • EBS Options:*
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] - gp2/gp3: ~$0.08/GB-month (general purpose)
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] - sc1 (Cold HDD): ~$0.015/GB-month (cheapest, but slow)
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] • Instance Store:*
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] - Ephemeral storage: $0/GB-month (free, but lost on stop/terminate)
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] • Alternative:*
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] - Smaller EBS + S3: Keep active data on EBS, archive to S3
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] For frequently accessed data, gp3 is your best balance of cost/performance. For archival data, sc1
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] or S3 are much cheaper.
2025-09-29 10:33:58 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:36 [nitro@ryzen5] how big can ephemeral be?
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] Ephemeral storage size depends on EC2 instance type:
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] • Small instances:*
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - t4g.nano: None
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - t4g.micro: None
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - t4g.small: None
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] • Medium instances:*
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - m6g.large: 1 x 75 GB NVMe
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - c6g.large: 1 x 118 GB NVMe
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] • Large instances:*
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - i4i.large: 1 x 468 GB NVMe
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - r6gd.large: 1 x 118 GB NVMe
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] • Maximum:*
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] - i4i.32xlarge: 1 x 30,000 GB NVMe (30TB)
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] Check the instance type specs - not all instances include instance store, and sizes vary
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] significantly.
2025-09-29 10:34:45 [deepseek-chat] 
