2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] It’s a raw-mode terminal input handler with these standout bits:
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - Grapheme-aware cursor motion (wide-char safe, handles tabs, emoji).
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - Inline short-code expansion (`:heart:` → ❤️) while you type.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - Concurrent TCP listener on 8081 for “slop” clients; interleaves remote commands/messages with
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] local keystrokes.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - UTF-8 re-assembly for partial sequences, back-space that re-writes the whole line, and a tiny
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] ANSI state-machine.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] - Non-blocking prompt: returns either `{line}` or `{messages}` (remote), so the host can mix CLI
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] and network traffic without extra threads.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] No obvious bugs, but two small notes:
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. `receivePromises` keeps growing if peers disconnect dirty; you may want to prune on socket
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] close.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. The `onCSI` stub logs unknown sequences—fine for now, but if you add more CSI handlers remember
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] to update the return offset `n`.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] Neat piece of kit—gives fountain a proper telnet-style repl without giving up local editing
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] comforts.
2025-10-11 09:26:04 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:26:30 [nitro@ryzen5] pasting of unicode is broken when using slopprmpt mode
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] The raw-mode paste problem is that the terminal sends the whole Unicode string in one `read()`
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] burst, but your UTF-8 re-assembler only looks at single-byte boundaries inside that burst.
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Any multi-byte sequence that is split across two `read()` calls is lost; you decode the first
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] fragment alone, see an invalid sequence, and drop it.
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] Keep a tiny carry-over buffer between reads and feed the concatenated bytes to the
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] decoder.
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]  // near the top
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +let utf8carry = new Uint8Array(4);   // max 4 bytes for UTF-8
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +let carryLen  = 0;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]  // inside the main read loop, replace the UTF-8 block with:
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    // 1. append carry-over bytes (if any) to current chunk
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    const chunk = new Uint8Array(carryLen + n);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    chunk.set(utf8carry.subarray(0, carryLen), 0);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    chunk.set(value, carryLen);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    // 2. decode as much as possible
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]      let i = 0;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] -    while (i < n) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] -        const byte = value[i];
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    while (i < chunk.length) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +        const byte = chunk[i];
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          // existing header-byte logic stays identical
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          if (byte >= 0xC0) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]              let need = 0;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]              if ((byte & 0xE0) === 0xC0) need = 2;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]              else if ((byte & 0xF0) === 0xE0) need = 3;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]              else if ((byte & 0xF8) === 0xF0) need = 4;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] -            if (i + need <= n) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] -                const seq = value.subarray(i, i + need);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +            if (i + need <= chunk.length) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +                const seq = chunk.subarray(i, i + need);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                  try {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                      const char = decoder.decode(seq);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                      bytes.push(...encoder.encode(char));
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                      addInput(char);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                      i += need;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                      continue;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]                  } catch { /* fall through */ }
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]              }
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          }
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          // single-byte or invalid – handle as before
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          ...existing single-byte code...
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]          i++;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905]      }
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    // 3. save incomplete tail for next read
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    carryLen = 0;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    if (i < chunk.length) {
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +        const tail = chunk.subarray(i);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +        utf8carry.set(tail);
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +        carryLen = tail.length;
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] +    }
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] With this three-step pattern every UTF-8 sequence is re-assembled across read boundaries and paste
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] (or fast typing) of any Unicode character works reliably.
2025-10-11 09:27:03 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:06 [nitro@ryzen5] on the other hand
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] On the other hand, if you’d rather not keep a manual carry-over buffer you can let the built-in
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] streaming decoder do the re-assembly for you.
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] The trick is to always feed the raw byte chunk to the same `TextDecoder` instance
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] and never try to slice UTF-8 sequences yourself.
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] Replace the whole UTF-8 block with two lines:
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] // 1. pump every byte into the streaming decoder
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] const text = rxDecoder.decode(value, { stream: true }); // may end mid-sequence
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] // 2. handle the characters that are complete *right now*
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] for (const ch of [...segmenter.segment(text)].map(s => s.segment)) {
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (ch.codePointAt(0) === 0x7F || ch === '\b') {
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         backspace(bytes);
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     } else if (ch === '\x1b') {
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         ...existing escape handling...
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     } else if (ch === '\r' || ch === '\n') {
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         bytes.push(0x0D, 0x0A);
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         busy = false;
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     } else {
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         bytes.push(...encoder.encode(ch));
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         addInput(ch);
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]         if (ch === ':') { ...shortcode logic... }
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] `rxDecoder` (already declared as `{ stream: true }`) keeps any incomplete sequence in its internal
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] buffer and finishes it on the next `read()`.
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] No extra state, no byte juggling, and paste of arbitrary Unicode becomes bullet-proof.
2025-10-11 09:43:30 [kimi-k2-0905] 
