2025-10-11 10:00:02 [nitro@ryzen5] can we fix it so it doesn appear as diamond ?
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] The “diamond” (�) is the replacement character your terminal shows when it receives a UTF-8
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] sequence it can’t display.
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] The three zero-width spaces are already valid Unicode, so the bytes are fine; the problem is the
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] font/glyph coverage of the console you’re using.
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] Short of changing the terminal font, the easiest fix is to strip the three U+200B characters before
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] the line is echoed. Add one helper and call it in the two places `slopPrompt` writes to the screen.
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] // slopprompt.ts
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] function stripZWSP(s: string): string {
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return s.replace(/\u200B/g, '');
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] In `slopPrompt`, wrap the two echo sites:
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] await writer.write(encoder.encode(harden(stripZWSP(message), 1e6)));
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] and
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] await writer.write(encoder.encode(stripZWSP(harden(prompt + line, 8e6))));
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing else needs to change; the characters are still preserved in the actual input buffer, they
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] just never hit the terminal, so no diamond appears.
2025-10-11 10:00:18 [kimi-k2-0905] 
