2025-10-11 10:22:34 [nitro@ryzen5] why is it
2025-10-11 10:22:35 [nitro@ryzen5] 						i += bytesNeeded - 1; // Skip the extra bytes
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] It’s how the decoder keeps its place in the byte stream.
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] UTF-8 encodes one Unicode character with 1–4 bytes.
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] After the first byte you already know how many more are coming (`bytesNeeded-1`).
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] If you didn’t skip them the next loop iteration would try to interpret those continuation bytes as
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] brand-new characters and you’d get garbage.
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] So the line just advances the index past the bytes that have already been consumed for the current
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] character.
2025-10-11 10:22:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
