2025-10-21 15:01:03 [nitro@ryzen5] i wish i could paste moji in sloppyprompt
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] kimi-k2-0905-preview: That would be a nice touch! Looking at your `slopprompt.ts`, the raw-mode
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] input loop is already Unicode-aware (it uses `Intl.Segmenter` to split grapheme clusters and
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] `TextDecoder` with `{stream:true}`), so the low-level plumbing can accept emoji just fine.
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] The blocker is the paste path: when you paste, the terminal emulator sends the bytes as if
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] you had typed them character-by-character.  If the paste contains a multi-byte emoji, the first
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] byte arrives, `slopPrompt` tries to decode it immediately, gets an incomplete sequence, and either:
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. treats it as garbage and drops it, or
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. (worse) tries to `JSON.parse` it later and throws the “JSON error” you saw in your notes.
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick workaround that keeps the raw feel:
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Detect a paste burst (many chars within one event-loop tick).
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Buffer the whole burst, decode it as a single unit, then replay the resulting string through the
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] normal input handler.
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] One-liner sketch inside the `readPromise` loop:
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] const t0 = performance.now();
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] const burst = [];
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] while (performance.now() - t0 < 16) {          // 16 ms ≈ one frame
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   const { value, done } = await reader.read();
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   if (done) break;
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   burst.push(...value);
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] if (burst.length > 1) {                          // smells like paste
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   const text = new TextDecoder("utf-8", {fatal:false}).decode(Uint8Array.from(burst));
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   for (const ch of [...segmenter.segment(text)]) addInput(ch.segment);
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] } else {
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905]   // single-byte path you already have
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] That keeps cursor movement, backspace, etc. snappy, but lets emoji (and any other multi-byte
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] sequence) land intact when pasted.
2025-10-21 15:01:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
