2025-10-29 13:38:54 [nitro@ryzen5] the pasting of unicode layer 2 on windows sloppy prompt still an issue
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] The Windows paste issue is a known limitation with how slopprompt handles wide
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] unicode input. The current grapheme clustering in slopprompt.ts works for most cases but breaks
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] when Windows clipboard injects variation selectors or combines emoji in unexpected ways.
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] Quick fix: In `slopprompt.ts`, modify the `addInput()` function to normalize pasted content before
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] processing:
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] function addInput(input: string) {
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905]     // Normalize Windows clipboard quirks
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905]     const normalized = input.normalize("NFC").replace(/\uFE0F/g, "");
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905]     grapheme.push(...[...segmenter.segment(normalized)].map(segment => segment.segment));
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] This strips the problematic variation selectors that Windows adds. For deeper unicode paste
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] handling, consider implementing a paste detection routine that buffers clipboard content separately
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] from keyboard input.
2025-10-29 13:39:07 [kimi-k2-0905] 
