2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] The normalization approach is correct, but there's a syntax error with the double
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] dots. Here's the corrected function:
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] function addInput(raw: string) {
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const input = raw.normalize("NFC").replace(/\uFE0F/g, "");
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 	grapheme.push(...[...segmenter.segment(input)].map(segment => segment.segment));
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] This should handle Windows clipboard unicode better by:
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Normalizing to NFC form
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Removing variation selectors that Windows loves to inject
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Using the segmenter for proper grapheme boundaries
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] The commented regex approaches were overkill - the segmenter handles the unicode boundary detection
2025-10-29 13:40:42 [kimi-k2-0905] more reliably across platforms.
