2025-10-29 15:20:18 [nitro@ryzen5] 				const status=statusChar+modelSpec.join(" ")+" ";
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] Yes — use a Unicode "fatter" space. Common choices:
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - Em space: "\u2003" (very wide)
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - En space: "\u2002" (half em)
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - Figure space: "\u2007" (monospace digit-width)
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - Thin / Hair space (you already have ThinSpace = "\u2009", HairSpace = "\u200A")
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] Example — define a named constant and use it in your join:
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] const FatterSpace = "\u2003"; // EM SPACE
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] const status = statusChar + modelSpec.join(FatterSpace) + FatterSpace;
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] If you need multiple wide gaps:
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] const gap = "\u2003".repeat(2);
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] const status = statusChar + modelSpec.join(gap) + gap;
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] 
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] Notes
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - Terminal rendering varies by font/terminal; an EM SPACE may still count as a single character for
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] your stringWidth function. If you rely on stringWidth for alignment, you may need to treat the
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] chosen space as wider there (e.g. add a special-case for codepoint 0x2003 to count as width 2).
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] - Prefer a named constant (FatterSpace) so the meaning is clear and easy to change across the
2025-10-29 15:20:36 [gpt-5-mini] codebase.
