2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] [gemini-2.5-05] I see the issue: the Gemini model is triggering the `readtime` function
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] repeatedly in a loop, likely because the `toolcall` response is being appended back to the
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] history in a way that the model interprets as needing to call the function again.
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] The fix is to exclude the tool-calling turn itself from the messages you send back to
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] Gemini. When a model generates a `tool_call`, you should:
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 1. Execute the tool.
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 2. Append the tool result to the history (not the tool call itself).
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 3. Skip adding the tool call to the history as a separate message.
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] In your Gemini payload preparation (`prepareGeminiContent`), you’re likely including the original
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] `tool_calls` in the history, causing Gemini to re-process them.
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] Modify your history filtering to omit any message that contains `tool_calls` when sending
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] the payload to Gemini. Only include the final tool result.
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] Example (pseudo-fix, adjust to your code):
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] // In prepareGeminiContent, filter out tool_call messages
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] const messages = payload.messages.filter(m => !m.tool_calls);
2025-10-29 17:07:01 [kimi-k2-0711] 
