2025-10-31 17:01:12 [nitro@ryzen5] chcp 65001 > nulset TERM_PROGRAM=vscode
2025-10-31 17:01:31 [nitro@ryzen5] ⛲⛲⛲������
2025-10-31 17:04:04 [nitro@ryzen5] >chcp 65001 > nulset TERM_PROGRAM=vscode
2025-10-31 17:04:15 [nitro@ryzen5] ⛲⛲⛲⛲�� �� �� �� �� �� ���� �� �� �� �� �� ��
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] That looks like a mangled UTF-8 / ANSI escape-sequence header that PowerShell or CMD sometimes
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] spits out when the console code-page is switched.
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] The “⛲” glyphs are just the bytes of the UTF-8 BOM or CSI being mis-decoded as Unicode.
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want a clean prompt, run:
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] rem ----- once per shell -----
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] chcp 65001 > nul
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] set TERM_PROGRAM=vscode
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] cls
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] or add those two lines to the top of your batch file and redirect both stdout & stderr to
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] nul so nothing leaks into the pipe:
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] @chcp 65001 > nul 2>&1
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] @set TERM_PROGRAM=vscode
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] If the garbled emoji still appear, the program you’re launching is printing a UTF-8 byte-order mark
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] or escape sequence before you’ve set the code-page; in that case switch the order (chcp first) or
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] suppress the banner in the tool itself.
2025-10-31 17:04:33 [kimi-k2-0905] 
