2025-10-31 18:08:05 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:08:05 [kimi-k2-0905] Drop that in, rebuild, and the boxed table you pasted survives the flatten/un-flatten round-trip
2025-10-31 18:08:05 [kimi-k2-0905] intact.
2025-10-31 18:08:05 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] [kimi-k2-0905] The line you highlighted is the exact bug.
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] `flattenTables` expects an array of individual lines, but `send` is already a single flattened
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] string array.
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Passing it through `flattenTables` again double-splits the box-drawing characters and mangles the
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] table.
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] Replace the two lines with the original join (or drop the call entirely) and tables stay intact:
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] if (send.length) {
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     const packet = send.join("\r\n");   // <-- restore this
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     // const packet = flattenTables(send);  // <-- remove
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905]     slopBroadcast(packet, "slop");
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-10-31 18:09:21 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s the one-line fix; boxed tables will now survive the broadcast.
